# Splunk Architecture Capstone Project

## Overview

This project documents the build and configuration of a distributed Splunk environment hosted on AWS. The architecture includes an indexer cluster, a search head cluster, centralized deployment components, and a Universal Forwarder used to collect and forward log data.


> **Security note:** Credentials, shared secrets, and environment-specific IP addresses have been replaced with placeholders in this public version. Do not commit real passwords, `pass4SymmKey` values, private keys, or other secrets to a public GitHub repository.

## Architecture Components

The lab uses the following Splunk roles:

- **Cluster Manager (CM)** - manages the indexer cluster.
- **Indexers (IDX)** - receive, index, replicate, and store data.
- **Deployer (DP)** - distributes apps and configuration bundles to the search head cluster.
- **Search Heads (SH)** - provide the search and investigation layer.
- **Deployment Server (DS)** - centrally manages apps and configurations for Universal Forwarders.
- **Universal Forwarder (UF)** - collects logs from the monitored Linux host and forwards them to the indexer tier.

The AWS build documented in the source used Amazon Linux instances. Most Splunk Enterprise nodes used `t2.medium` instances with 16 GiB gp3 root volumes, while the Universal Forwarder host used a `t3.micro` instance with an 8 GiB gp3 root volume.

## Network Ports Used in the Lab

The source project documented the following ports in the AWS security group:

| Port | Documented Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `8000` | Splunk Web |
| `8080` | Indexer replication |
| `9887` | Indexer replication |
| `8081` | Search head replication |
| `8181` | Search head replication |
| `8088` | HTTP Event Collector (HEC) |
| `8191` | KV Store replication |
| `9997` | Splunk receiving port for Universal Forwarders |
| `9998` | Documented SSL receiving port for Universal Forwarders |

> **Important:** This table preserves the ports recorded in the original capstone. Before using this design outside the lab, validate the required ports against the Splunk configuration you actually deploy and restrict AWS security-group sources appropriately. Avoid exposing management, replication, ingestion, or Splunk Web ports broadly to the internet.

